Q: Mono vs Flux in Spring WebFlux / Project Reactor?

Both are reactive types from **Project Reactor** (used by Spring WebFlux).
They represent **async, non-blocking** data pipelines — not the data itself until subscribed.

────────────────────────────────────────
One-liner

**Mono** = 0 or 1 result (like `Optional` / single Future).  
**Flux** = 0..N results (like async Stream / list over time).

────────────────────────────────────────
1) Mono<T>

- Publishes **at most one** item, then completes (or errors)
- Use for: findById, save, HTTP call returning one body, void-like ops (`Mono<Void>`)

Mono<User> user = userRepository.findById(1L);
Mono<String> name = Mono.just("Afham");
Mono<Void> done = Mono.fromRunnable(() -> log.info("ok"));

────────────────────────────────────────
2) Flux<T>

- Publishes **0 to many** items
- Use for: list all, streaming, SSE, multiple DB rows, kafka-like streams

Flux<User> users = userRepository.findAll();
Flux<Integer> nums = Flux.just(1, 2, 3);
Flux<String> ticks = Flux.interval(Duration.ofSeconds(1)).map(String::valueOf);

────────────────────────────────────────
Compare

| Feature        | Mono              | Flux                |
| -------------- | ----------------- | ------------------- |
| Cardinality    | 0..1              | 0..N                |
| Like           | Optional / Future | Stream / List async |
| Typical API    | getById, save     | findAll, stream     |
| Spring WebFlux | single JSON body  | collection / stream |

────────────────────────────────────────
Important reactive rules (interview)

1) Nothing runs until **subscribe** (or WebFlux framework subscribes for you).
2) Operators are lazy: `map`, `flatMap`, `filter`, `zip`…
3) Backpressure: consumer can signal how much it can handle (Flux especially).
4) Don’t block inside reactive chain (`Thread.sleep`, JDBC without boundedElastic) — kills the model.
5) Error channel: `onErrorResume`, `onErrorReturn` instead of only try/catch around sync code.

────────────────────────────────────────
Tiny examples

// Mono → transform
Mono<String> upper = Mono.just("afham").map(String::toUpperCase);

// Flux → filter + map
Flux<Integer> evenSquares = Flux.range(1, 5)
    .filter(i -> i % 2 == 0)
    .map(i -> i * i);  // 4, 16

// Combine
Mono<User> user = ...;
Mono<Account> account = ...;
Mono<Dto> dto = Mono.zip(user, account, Dto::new);

// flatMap (async nested calls)
Mono<Order> order = userIdMono
    .flatMap(id -> orderClient.getOrder(id));

map = sync transform  
flatMap = async nested Publisher (avoid blocking)

────────────────────────────────────────
Spring MVC vs WebFlux

| Spring MVC (servlet)     | WebFlux              |
| ------------------------ | -------------------- |
| blocking threads per req | event-loop style     |
| return `User` / `List`   | return `Mono`/`Flux` |
| Tomcat thread per request | Netty (typically)   |

Use WebFlux when you need high concurrency with non-blocking IO.
If your code is mostly blocking JDBC, plain MVC + thread pool is often simpler.

────────────────────────────────────────
Quarkus mapping (you studied Quarkus)

| Reactor (Spring) | Mutiny (Quarkus) |
| ---------------- | ---------------- |
| `Mono<T>`        | `Uni<T>`         |
| `Flux<T>`        | `Multi<T>`       |

Same idea: one vs many async results — different API names.

────────────────────────────────────────
Interview answer

“Mono is a reactive publisher for 0 or 1 element — good for single-resource APIs. Flux is for 0 to N elements — lists and streams.
In Spring WebFlux we return these types so the framework can subscribe in a non-blocking way. They’re similar to Quarkus Mutiny’s Uni and Multi.”
